<description>
Fill the small background compartments bounded by the long contour color.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color and two foreground colors.
- The more frequent foreground color forms long 8-connected contour fragments.
- The other foreground color is a fixed distractor/obstacle color that partitions
  the background but is never recolored.

Rule:
- Split the background into 4-connected regions using all foreground pixels as
  barriers.
- Keep the two largest background regions as the outside of the contour drawing.
- Recolor every other background region that touches a long contour component
  to the contour color; leave all foreground pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
The apparent area fill is exactly a component-level fill of bounded background
compartments, not a row-wise interpolation between individual contour pixels.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "4-connected background regions partitioned by long 8-connected contour-color components and obstacle-color pixels",
  "transform": "recolor each non-exterior background region touching a long contour component to the contour color; keep the two largest background regions and all foreground pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30 [observed]
  colors: 3 distinct — 1 background (dominant), 1 contour, 1 obstacle
  contour_color: more frequent foreground; 9-12% of grid pixels
  obstacle_color: less frequent foreground; 4-7% of grid pixels
  long_contour_components:
    count: 2-3 (8-connected components spanning >=H/3 rows)
    shape: thin meandering 8-connected paths, ~1-2 pixels per row, present in every row of their span
    together_enclose: a region whose 4-connected bg compartments are filled in output
  short_contour_components:
    count: 3-14 isolated fragments (size 1-4, row span <H/3)
    role: visual distractors, do not participate in fill logic
  obstacle_pixels: scattered mostly as singletons or small clusters (size 1-5, 4-connected)
    distributed both inside and outside the contour enclosure
  background_regions: treating all foreground as barriers, 4-connected bg splits into 8-14 regions
    exterior: exactly 2 largest regions (clearly separated in size from interior ones)
    interior: 6-12 regions, sizes 1-57; those touching a long contour component get filled
  transform: fill each interior bg region adjacent to a long contour component with contour_color;
    all foreground pixels and exterior bg unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose 3 distinct colors for bg, contour, obstacle.
    2. On a 30x30 grid, draw 2-3 thin meandering 8-connected contour paths spanning most rows.
       Each path steps diagonally or has short horizontal runs, covering nearly every row.
       Together they partition the bg into 2 large exterior regions and 6-12 interior pockets.
    3. Fill all interior bg pockets (4-connected, touching a long contour component) with contour_color
       to produce the output grid.
    4. Scatter obstacle pixels (4-7% of grid) as singletons/small clusters inside and outside the enclosure.
       Obstacle pixels sit on bg in both input and output (they are never recolored).
    5. Add 3-5 short contour fragments (size 1-4) outside the main enclosure as distractors.
    6. To derive the input, take the output and revert all filled interior bg pixels back to bg color.
       Contour, obstacle, and exterior bg pixels remain unchanged.
